# The newest interpreter every dependency installs on, and the version
# requires-python names. Not 3.15, though its release candidate counts:
# `rocksdict` publishes no wheel a 3.15 interpreter installs and no
# sdist, so the lock does not install there, and the pin rises once it
# publishes either. Not 3.14t: that is this same version with the GIL
# off, not a second one, and nothing here asks for the GIL off by
# default. Node.worker_pool builds a ThreadPool rather than a process
# Pool where sys._is_gil_enabled() is False (btclib-org/btclib-node#388,
# building on btclib-org/btclib-node#385), but choosing that at runtime
# is not the same as switching this interpreter's own default over to
# 3.14t. Asking for it would also cost: `rocksdict`, the store
# (btclib-org/btclib-node#641), ships cp314 wheels and no cp314t wheel,
# so the lock does not install on 3.14t. The other compiled extensions
# of the runtime closure, `btclib-secp256k1` and `cffi` under btclib's
# `secp256k1` extra, ship paired cp314/cp314t wheels and do not
# re-enable the GIL: btclib-org/btclib-node#361 measured
# `sys._is_gil_enabled()` still False after importing both under
# 3.14.6+freethreaded. `librt` is a dependency of mypy, a
# development-only package and not part of the runtime closure at all.
# btclib-org/btclib-node#112 asks whether 3.14t is worth it, and this
# comment is the answer.
# A trailing comment on the version line below would make uv ignore this
# file, so the explanation stays up here instead.
3.14
